# behavior: a dependency's declarations keep one identity through every module that republishes them: a type published at its declaring module, at a directory facade and at the package root is one type to its consumer, a value spelled through one path is accepted where another path's type is expected, a model reached only as another model's list element has its fields and methods even when the root publishes a namesake, and a pattern names a type imported after the returning signature (#1561); a module that re-exports a dependency's renamed function under a further name hands consumers one callable name (#1751); root and nested modules re-export functions, models, enums and constants through aliases and facade chains (#1561); a Loaf re-exports another Loaf's model and enum after three source links, and their field, method and variant resolve through both plain and aliased imports (#2675); a library calls a sibling module through a bare module import while its script shares that module (#2237); standard-library declarations keep identity through transitive public aliases (#2510); and published ordinary, generic and type-token overloads retain selected identities and precise local result types through source bridges and aliases (#1033, #2514)
